{
  "teamId": 117,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 Houston Astros arrived with championship expectations and spent the entire season justifying them. After a modest 11-10 April that masked genuine star power — Jeremy Peña announcing himself as Carlos Correa's worthy heir with a go-ahead two-run bomb, and Michael Brantley's veteran touch showing up in the clutch — the club caught fire in May. Going 22-8 that month, the Astros established themselves as the class of the American League, with the pitching staff and a deep, balanced lineup punishing opponents night after night.\n\nJune and July brought the Yankees to town and a reminder that even elite teams bleed. Ryan Pressly and the bullpen had some costly moments — a three-run Aaron Hicks homer, a DJ LeMahieu blast, a pair of Judge shots over two days — but the Astros kept winning anyway, going 35-18 over those two months. July 4th saw Yordan Alvarez launch a solo shot into the Houston night as if to declare the summer belonged to them, and Jeremy Peña kept stacking moments that silenced any doubt about the shortstop position.\n\nAugust tested the depth of the roster. The bullpen continued to spring leaks, and the White Sox and Braves took turns inflicting damage, but grand slams from Aledmys Díaz and Kyle Tucker showed that this lineup could answer any punch. The Astros finished August 17-11, good enough, and closed the regular season 18-8 in September — Yuli Gurriel and Kyle Tucker providing a late-month reminder of just how many ways this team could hurt you.\n\nThe postseason was Yordan Alvarez's coronation. He ended the ALDS against Seattle with a walk-off three-run blast of almost mythological force, then added another homer in the clincher. Peña homered too, cementing his Rookie of the Year case on baseball's biggest stage. After dispatching the Yankees in the ALCS, the Astros met the Phillies in the World Series. Philadelphia pushed back — J.T. Realmuto's home run off Luis García in Game 1 set an early tone — but the Astros were too complete, too experienced, too loaded. Alvarez delivered a three-run shot in the clinching game, and Houston had its second World Series title in six years.",
  "chapters": [
    {
